---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `let price = 19.99;
let quantity = 3;
console.log("Total:", price * quantity;`;

const Bug2 = `let score1 = 88;
let score2 = 92;
let score3 = 79;
console.log("Average:", score1 + score2 + score3 / 3);`;

const Bug3 = `let totalMinutes = 200;
let wholeHours = totalMinutes % 60;
console.log("Whole hours:", wholeHours);`;

const Bug4 = `let crewCount = 7;
console.log("=== ROSTER ===");
console.log("Crew aboard:", crewcount);`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Arithmetic</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Four short programs. Every one of them is broken. Find the mistake,
            say <i>what kind</i> of mistake it is, and fix it.
          </p>

          <p>
            You did not write these, which is the point. Reading someone else's
            broken code is a different skill from writing your own, and it is
            the one you will use most &mdash; including on the AP exam, where
            you will be asked to describe a change that would break a program.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Reading an error message and finding what it points at</li>
            <li>Telling a broken program from a wrong program</li>
            <li>Order of operations</li>
            <li>
              Choosing between <b>%</b> and <b>/</b>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="kinds-of-mistakes">
          <h2>Three Kinds of Mistakes</h2>

          <p>
            Not every bug looks the same, and the difference changes how you
            hunt for it.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              A <b class="text-sn-theme">syntax error</b> means JavaScript
              cannot read your program at all. Nothing runs.
            </li>
            <li>
              A <b class="text-sn-theme">reference error</b> means it read the
              program fine, started running, and then hit a name it has never
              heard of. Part of your output may appear before it stops.
            </li>
            <li>
              A <b class="text-sn-theme">logic error</b> means it read your
              program, ran the whole thing, and did exactly what you told it
              &mdash; but what you told it was wrong. It finishes, it prints
              something, and the something is incorrect.{" "}
              <b>There is no error message at all.</b>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Logic errors are the dangerous ones, because nothing warns you. The
            only way to catch them is to know what the answer <i>should</i> be
            and notice that it isn't.
          </p>

          <h4 class="mt-4">One Warning About Line Numbers</h4>

          <p>
            When JavaScript reports a syntax error, it names the line where it{" "}
            <i>noticed</i> something was wrong &mdash; not always the line where
            the mistake is. Those are often different, and sometimes it blames a
            line past the end of the file.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat the line number as a starting point, not an answer. Look at
            the line it names, then look <i>upward</i>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <p>For each one, run it. Then write down:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Which kind of mistake &mdash; <b>syntax</b>, <b>reference</b>, or{" "}
              <b>logic</b>
            </li>
            <li>What is actually wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p><b>Symptom:</b> this program refuses to run at all.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 1"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Extra question, and it is the important one:{" "}
            <b>
              which line number does JavaScript blame, and is that where the
              mistake actually is?
            </b>
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> it runs and prints{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-red">Average: 206.33333333333334</b>. The average
            of 88, 92, and 79 should be about{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-green">86.33</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 2"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Hint: the number is far too big. Which part of that line does
            JavaScript do <i>first</i>?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> it runs and prints{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-red">Whole hours: 20</b>. There are{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-green">3</b> whole hours in 200 minutes.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 3"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The operator being used is not wrong in general &mdash; it is just
            answering a different question than the one asked. What question is
            it answering?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> the heading prints, and then it stops with an
            error. Note that <i>some</i> output appeared before it died &mdash;
            that alone tells you this is not a syntax error.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 4"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Read the declaration and the usage character by character.
            JavaScript does not think those are the same word.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For each of the four bugs, submit:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              The kind of mistake &mdash; syntax, reference, or logic
            </li>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line of code</li>
          </ul>

          <p>Plus your answer to Bug 1's extra question about the line number.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Two of these four are logic errors.</b> If you found yourself
            waiting for an error message on those, that is exactly the habit
            this challenge is meant to break.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
